# World model types

The value vocabulary the world model holds. Where those values live — nodes,
offsets, pages, the store — is `research/persistence.dj`'s business. What a value
*is* is this file.

## Ion's data model, without Ion's serialization

Amazon Ion is a *serialization format*: a set of data types, a textual notation
and a binary notation for them, "semantically isomorphic" across the two.
<https://amazon-ion.github.io/ion-docs/>

We take the data model — the types — and neither notation, because the data is
used foremost in memory: the in-memory structure is the model and the file is a
rendering of it (`research/persistence.dj`). An encoding chosen for a stream
(length-prefixing, symbol tables, text escapes) is not one chosen for a mapped
image, so nothing below is about bytes.

## The types

Ion's model is "a set of primitive types and a set of recursively-defined
container types", every type supporting a null and user-defined annotations.

| type | spec | shape in memory |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `null` | one typed null per type, plus bare `null` | a null variant per tag |
| `bool` | true / false | bit |
| `int` | signed, arbitrary size | `i64` fast path, bignum variant |
| `float` | IEEE-754 binary, 32 or 64 bit | `f32` or `f64` |
| `decimal` | arbitrary precision, coefficient + exponent; trailing zeros significant; negative zero distinct | coefficient + exponent + sign |
| `timestamp` | year to fractional second, arbitrary precision; local offset required with time; `-00:00` means unknown offset | precision tag, fields, offset |
| `string` | Unicode text | UTF-8 bytes |
| `symbol` | interned, case-sensitive Unicode atom | a symbol-table id |
| `blob` | arbitrary bytes | bytes |
| `clob` | 7-bit-ASCII text with an application-declared encoding | bytes plus a charset — see below |
| `list` | ordered, heterogeneous | ordered child offsets |
| `struct` | unordered symbol-to-value pairs; repeated names preserved; readers may reorder | ordered fields — see below |
| `sexp` | ordered; "Ion does not define their interpretation" | ordered children — see below |

## Annotations are the tag

Any Ion value may carry an ordered list of annotation *symbol tokens* — "they do
not have annotations themselves" — applied as `tag::value`. This is exactly the
"a record has to say what it is — a tag and a version" of
`research/persistence.dj`, carried by the model rather than by an encoding: a
record's kind is an annotation, its version another, and both are interned ids.

## The one type Ion does not have

Ion's model is *value-based* and has no references: the spec says the data model
"does not include references" and that it "can express data hierarchies (we can
nest things to arbitrary depth), but not general directed graphs". The world is a directed
graph: a fork records its fork point on both sides and neither is complete alone
(`research/agent-world.dj`), and running prompts reference each other's turns
(`research/turns.dj`). So the model needs one kind Ion's set lacks — a
*reference*, an id or offset naming another node — and the world model is Ion's
types plus that.

It is also the kind the store needs anyway: a node names another by offset
(`research/persistence.dj`), so the value kind and the addressing scheme are the
same thing.

## What we drop, and why

- *`clob`.* It exists to carry non-UTF-8 text through a text format; with
  `string` being UTF-8 and `blob` being bytes, it is a third way to say one of two
  things. Kept only if a tool needs a declared non-UTF-8 charset.
- *`sexp`.* No semantics by construction, so it is a `list` with another tag
  unless something consumes expressions. Dropped until one does.
- *`struct`'s looseness.* Ion's struct is unordered, permits repeated field
  names, and lets readers reorder. A durable record wants a stable order and
  unique names, so the container is taken and the looseness dropped.
- *`float` and `decimal` both stay* only if the world carries exact reals; if it
  does not, `decimal` goes and `float` is the one real type.
- *Arbitrary-precision `int`* is Ion's, so every integer node carries a bignum
  path; the `i64` fast path is the mitigation, not a removal.
- *Typed `null`* is kept: a null variant per tag, not one null, because the
  distinction *is* the type.

## The result

A closed set — Ion's tags with their precision semantics, minus `clob` and `sexp`
unless needed, plus a reference. That is a Zig tagged union, and the union is the
schema (`research/persistence.dj`).